1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,950
Flip a fair coin four times.

2
00:00:02,070 --> 00:00:04,490
After each heads, check the next flip:

3
00:00:04,790 --> 00:00:06,750
here, one of the two is heads.

4
00:00:07,050 --> 00:00:10,270
Do this for every four flip run, and average.

5
00:00:10,570 --> 00:00:12,750
Below, at, or above one half?

6
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:18,270
One half feels obvious, since a coin has no memory.

7
00:00:18,570 --> 00:00:26,050
In nineteen eighty five, a study ran this kind of check on basketball shots, and called the hot hand an illusion.

8
00:00:26,666 --> 00:00:27,796
Heads are hits.

9
00:00:27,916 --> 00:00:32,216
A hot shooter beats their usual rate; a random one should match it.

10
00:00:32,666 --> 00:00:37,056
First, test the test on a shooter we know is random: a fair coin.

11
00:00:37,176 --> 00:00:41,206
Four flips can land sixteen ways, all equally likely.

12
00:00:41,666 --> 00:00:44,596
This row checks three flips, and two are heads.

13
00:00:46,033 --> 00:00:50,623
Two rows show no heads before the last flip, so they get no score.

14
00:00:51,066 --> 00:00:53,886
Drop the other fourteen scores onto the line.

15
00:00:54,186 --> 00:00:57,346
Their average is seventeen out of forty two.

16
00:00:57,933 --> 00:01:02,563
On a fair coin, the test reads cold, by nine and a half points.

17
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:05,360
Yet no flip is biased.

18
00:01:05,860 --> 00:01:10,280
Gather all twenty four checked flips: twelve heads, twelve tails.

19
00:01:10,580 --> 00:01:13,770
Total heads over total flips: exactly half.

20
00:01:14,366 --> 00:01:20,706
The skew comes from scoring row by row: each row gets one vote, however many flips it checked.

21
00:01:21,166 --> 00:01:25,806
Checked heads come in crowds, since a heads gets the next flip checked too.

22
00:01:26,306 --> 00:01:31,886
Here, three heads share a single vote, while this lone tails gets a whole one.

23
00:01:32,633 --> 00:01:34,903
Twelve heads crowd into eight rows.

24
00:01:35,203 --> 00:01:37,573
Twelve tails spread across eleven.

25
00:01:38,233 --> 00:01:40,093
Crowded heads split their votes.

26
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:41,950
Line them up:

27
00:01:42,750 --> 00:01:46,090
the heads hold five and two thirds of the fourteen votes.

28
00:01:47,090 --> 00:01:49,380
That's seventeen out of forty two.

29
00:01:50,833 --> 00:01:54,573
So the test takes an average of ratios, one per row,

30
00:01:54,873 --> 00:01:56,963
instead of the ratio of totals.

31
00:01:58,466 --> 00:02:00,946
Now score the flip after each tails.

32
00:02:01,246 --> 00:02:09,646
Swap heads and tails, and the strip flips over one half: the test reads high, twenty five out of forty two.

33
00:02:10,233 --> 00:02:14,313
Subtract the averages: a gap of minus nineteen points.

34
00:02:15,266 --> 00:02:25,496
The nineteen eighty five study ran the same comparison on twenty six Cornell players: up to a hundred shots each, from where they'd hit about half.

35
00:02:25,996 --> 00:02:29,186
After three hits, versus after three misses.

36
00:02:29,633 --> 00:02:38,643
For a random shooter, the same crowding pulls after hits down to forty six percent on average, and after misses up to fifty four.

37
00:02:39,093 --> 00:02:42,183
Miller and Sanjurjo's twenty eighteen paper

38
00:02:42,683 --> 00:02:45,073
puts the gap at about minus eight points.

39
00:02:45,973 --> 00:02:47,593
Longer records shrink it,

40
00:02:47,943 --> 00:02:49,893
but a hundred shots isn't long.

41
00:02:50,333 --> 00:02:57,683
The Cornell players hit forty nine percent after three hits, and forty five after three misses.

42
00:02:57,983 --> 00:03:03,643
The study found plus four, too close to zero to count, so no hot hand.

43
00:03:04,433 --> 00:03:07,563
Measured from minus eight, it's twelve points hot.

44
00:03:08,463 --> 00:03:13,913
That's evidence for a hot hand in a gym, not proof it survives defenders in a game.

45
00:03:15,500 --> 00:03:16,460
One half

46
00:03:16,610 --> 00:03:18,310
is what a fair coin does.

47
00:03:19,110 --> 00:03:22,960
Seventeen out of forty two is what this test sees.

48
00:03:23,260 --> 00:03:24,940
A fifty percent shooter

49
00:03:25,240 --> 00:03:27,490
who averages one half on this test

50
00:03:27,690 --> 00:03:29,380
is already running hot.

